WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.490
The CPU handles general computation, application logic, tokenization, and inference when no accelerator is used.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:17.056
CPU inference can be useful for smaller or highly quantized models, but memory bandwidth and core behavior influence performance.

00:00:17.136 --> 00:00:23.627
A GPU provides parallel compute and high-bandwidth memory suited to model operations.

00:00:23.707 --> 00:00:32.195
VRAM is memory on or available to the graphics processor for weights, caches, activations, and runtime overhead.

00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:36.769
Insufficient VRAM can force offload, reduced settings, or failure.

00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:44.339
System RAM holds the operating system, runtime, model data, CPU-resident layers, cache, and applications.

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:55.902
Unified-memory architectures share a memory pool between CPU and GPU, reducing some transfers but still operating within total capacity and bandwidth limits.

00:00:55.982 --> 00:01:02.473
Storage capacity and speed affect model downloads, startup, swaps, logs, datasets, and backups.

00:01:02.553 --> 00:01:09.044
Models can consume many gigabytes, and keeping several formats or quantizations multiplies requirements.

00:01:09.124 --> 00:01:13.618
Preserve enough free space for safe operation and updates.

00:01:13.698 --> 00:01:24.183
PCI Express connects discrete devices and can affect transfer behavior when layers or data move between system memory and GPU memory.

00:01:24.263 --> 00:01:32.252
Power supplies, cooling, chassis airflow, and electrical capacity determine whether peak performance can be sustained safely.

00:01:32.332 --> 00:01:36.327
Laptops trade portability for thermal and power constraints.

00:01:36.407 --> 00:01:39.902
Desktops are easier to cool and expand.

00:01:39.982 --> 00:01:48.969
Consumer hardware may provide strong value, while workstation or server hardware may add memory, reliability, management, and support.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:53.044
Match the platform to workload and operational expectations.
